=== reading ===
Both arms of the main experiment returned HTTP 200 with the same body and moved the counters
by the same 20. The switch gained 20 rows in one and 1 in the other. So `succeeded` counts
ACCEPTED PROGRAMMING OPERATIONS, not rules, and nothing in the response, the counters or
`recent_failures` distinguishes an add from a replace. The DISTINCT arm is the control that
gives the comparison its power: the counter CAN equal the row count, so a ratio of 20.00 is a
property of the workload and not of the instrument.

Consequence, stated exactly: from outside the switch you cannot derive how many rules exist.
The divergence equals the number of replaces and is unbounded -- 20 POSTs in 1.0 s produced a
20x gap; 1000 would produce 1000x. The only channel that knows is the switch itself.
